diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported 30–35% packet loss between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and the partner host pc2 (200.1.1.2). End-to-end connectivity is clean: bidirectional pings show 0% loss with normal payloads, with DF at MTU-sized probes (-s 1472 -M do), and even with oversized 2000-byte payloads (fragmented). Round-trip times are sub‑2 ms. FRR on router1/router2 has a stable eBGP peering and correct routes installed to 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24. All links report 10G full duplex and are UP. There are no firewall rules dropping traffic (iptables default ACCEPT; nftables contains only Docker loopback NAT). Interface/qdisc statistics on every hop show zero errors/drops/overlimits.

Mechanism and localization: If there had been real in-path loss, we would expect (a) ping loss, (b) tc/ethtool/interface error counters incrementing, or (c) ACL hits. None of these are present on pc1, router1 (eth1/eth0), router2 (eth0/eth1), or pc2. iperf shows good throughput (~140–150 Mbps) with a burst of early TCP retransmissions, which is consistent with host/VM scheduling or transient receiver window dynamics rather than persistent network loss.

Root cause: The earlier 1-in-3 “ping” loss cannot be confirmed now; it was likely transient or host/application-side behavior outside the network path.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the issue recurs, capture simultaneously on pc1:eth0 and router1:eth1 during the event to pinpoint any drops, and verify the application host is not CPU or disk bound. Continue to monitor; the routed path is healthy at this time.
=== END ===